Why environment files are excluded from Git, even in a “private” repository.

Even a private repository isn't truly guaranteed to stay private forever. This is because collaborators get added, repos get transferred, forks happen, or a repo intended to stay private can accidentally be flipped to public with a single wrong click. 
Also, anyone with legitimate access to the repo would be able to see values in a .env file that they dont need to see, such as AMI IDs, security group IDs, or actual credentials in a production setting. 
Git also keeps a full history of every commit, so even if a .env file is deleted later, its contents remain recoverable from earlier commits unless the entire history is rewritten.
Automated bots constantly search through GitHub. Even if they mostly target public repos, that risk grows the moment a repo's visibility changes or is misconfigured. 
Separating configuration from code also just makes the project more portable. Anyone cloning the repo should be able to supply their own .env based on a template. Instead of inheriting someone else's specific AWS resources. 
Overall, the .env.example file matters a lot. It matters because it documents what variables are needed without exposing the actual values behind them. 
